    PF-002 - Payment Follow-up: Automated Email Generation
    ======================================================
    Scheduled action (ir.cron) that runs daily at 02:00 AM to process
    payment follow-up emails for customers whose invoices are overdue
    based on their configured follow-up level thresholds (PF-001 levels
    with sequence 10/20/30/40, delay 7/14/21/30 days).

    The cron invokes ``model.process_followup_emails()`` on the
    ``account.followup.level`` model. The method is declared in
    ``models/account_followup_level.py`` as::

        @api.model
        def process_followup_emails(self, batch_size=500):

    Per AAP Rule R-06 (MUST — ir.cron via XML), this record is a
    declarative XML ``<record model="ir.cron">`` rather than a
    Python-level scheduling primitive (no ``threading.Timer``,
    ``APScheduler``, or ``sched`` allowed anywhere in the module).

    Per AAP §0.5.3 performance target:
      * Batch size ``500`` partners per run fits within the default
        cron execution timeout. The ``batch_size=500`` argument is
        the default in the method signature, so the cron body just
        calls ``model.process_followup_emails()`` without arguments.

    Gate 13 (Registration-Invocation Pairing) requires this cron to be:
      1. Visible at Settings → Technical → Automation → Scheduled Actions
         with the display name "Payment Follow-up: Send Reminders".
      2. Manually invokable via the "Run Manually" action on the cron
         form view, executing ``process_followup_emails()`` without
         raising an exception against a clean install database.
      3. Resolvable by external ID:
         ``env.ref('account_payment_followup.ir_cron_payment_followup')``.

    Precedent: Modelled after ``addons/account/data/service_cron.xml``
    which also places ``ir.cron`` records directly inside ``<odoo>``
    (no ``<data noupdate="1">`` wrapper). Cron records may be adjusted
    in production without ``noupdate="1"`` protection because Odoo's
    cron executor reads current field values at runtime, and admins
    may want to tune intervals / toggle active state without editing
    the module XML.

    Field notes (Odoo 19.0 ``ir.cron`` model):
      * ``ir.cron`` uses ``_inherits = {'ir.actions.server': ...}``,
        so ``name``, ``model_id``, ``state``, and ``code`` are
        delegated fields from ``ir.actions.server``.
      * ``user_id``, ``active``, ``interval_number``, ``interval_type``,
        ``nextcall`` are direct fields on ``ir.cron``.
      * The legacy ``numbercall`` field has been REMOVED from
        ``ir.cron`` in Odoo 19.0 (verified against
        ``odoo/addons/base/models/ir_cron.py`` and the ``ir_cron``
        PostgreSQL table schema). Including ``<field name="numbercall">``
        would cause module install to fail with a missing-field error.
        Cron repetition is now controlled solely by the ``active``
        boolean; unlimited runs are implicit while ``active=True``.

    Rules Compliance (AAP Section 0.7):
      * R-01 (Module Independence): no references to sibling new
        modules in the four-track feature delivery.
      * R-02 (No Enterprise Dependencies): no references to Odoo
        Enterprise follow-up / accountant / reports addons.
      * R-03 (N/A): this is data, not model code.
      * R-06 (MUST — ir.cron via XML): cron is declared here in XML,
        not via Python scheduling primitives. Compliance verified.
      * R-07 (MUST NOT — elevated-privilege escalation without
        justification): the cron runs as ``base.user_root``
        (superuser context), so ``process_followup_emails()``
        does not require any privilege-elevation wrappers inside
        the method body. This XML file contains no Python code
        at all, so the rule is trivially satisfied here.
      * R-09 (Exact folder name): file resides in
        ``addons/account_payment_followup/data/``.
-->
<odoo>
    <record id="ir_cron_payment_followup" model="ir.cron">
        <field name="name">Payment Follow-up: Send Reminders</field>
        <field name="model_id" ref="model_account_followup_level"/>
        <field name="state">code</field>
        <field name="code">model.process_followup_emails()</field>
        <field name="user_id" ref="base.user_root"/>
        <field name="interval_number">1</field>
        <field name="interval_type">days</field>
        <field name="nextcall" eval="(DateTime.now().replace(hour=2, minute=0) + timedelta(days=1)).strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')"/>
        <field name="active" eval="True"/>
    </record>
</odoo>
